         else:\n                        res = max(res, dp(i+1, nm, False))\n            return res\n        return dp(0,0,True)\nThis is a common template. It likely TLE? But it\'s an accepted answer maybe due to state small and LC tests. However as ChatGPT final should pass all tests including hidden. Need deliver accepted.\n\nLet\'s find a more optimized algorithm.\n\nAlternative dynamic programming by k cases.\n\nObservation: k=1 special easy. For k >? Maximum partitions after one change maybe can be computed from original partitions and local improvements. But changing can propagate. However perhaps the maximum additional partitions from one change is at most 1? No examples k=1 "xxyz" original partitions? k=1 original: "xx" (2 x), "y", "z" =>3. Change to xayz => partitions "x","a","y","z" =4 (+1). Could be more? For k=1, changing one char can split one run into at most two, adding at most 1 partition. For k>1, changing one char can create at most one additional boundary? Let\'s investigate. In general, changing one character in greedy segmentation can increase number of partitions by more than 1? Example k=2, s with long segment containing exactly 2 chars. Change middle to new char: previously entire long segment one partition. After change, first part up to before new char has <=2, then new char added would be 3rd => cut before change? Actually if change to new char, when scanning original two chars plus new, at changed char distinct becomes3 -> boundary before changed char. Then new partition starts with new char; following original two chars add to new char; if k=2, can include one old char, then second old char distinct third? New char + two olds =3; if both old chars appear, boundary soon. Could create more partitions. Example s="aabb", k=2. Original one partition. Change index1 a->c: "acbb". Greedy: scan a,c,b? k=2: "ac", then b would be 3rd? chars {a,c}+b -> cut before pos2? Let\'s compute s= a c b b. Longest prefix <=2 distinct: "ac" (a,c), next b causes