Also consider change to same char; get_end handles unchanged; total should equal original count from L. We can initialize best=original_count.\n\nWill this correctly account for modifications where c equals old and p>=start? old==c no change, get_end returns original_right[start]. If start L original segment containing p, original_right[L] = R > p unless p at? Since p in [L,R), R>p. r>p, total=pre+1+count_from[R] = original. Good.\nIf p maybe at segment end? p<R.\n\nAt most one change; we evaluate changing every p to every c, includes original and all. Max answer.\n\nNow need verify theorem max increase at most 2 not actually needed by this algorithm? We are evaluating actual result for each single change, but simulation stops after changed char left (r>p) and uses original suffix. Is it always true after r>p (boundary after modified char) no further influence? Yes because changed char at p is in segment [start,r). It is "left behind"; remaining string from r onward is entirely original s[r:]. Future segmentation starts fresh at r. So using original_count_from[r] correct. Even if first segment extends past p but boundary r>p, changed char included and left. All subsequent chars original. Segmentation from r is exactly original greedy from r (since no modifications for indices >=r). So exact.\n\nThe loop processes until r>p. Before that, if r<=p, changed char not included; remaining from r still contains p (unless r=p, p is next). We continue. Could r<p and start advance, still p ahead; eventually process. Since s before p unchanged, if starting L is original segment start containing p, first r should not be <p. But even if we evaluate p not within segment? We use L containing p, so okay. For general safety, could there be multiple boundaries before p under modified? Modification at p can\'t affect before p, and original segmentation from L before p? Wait L is start of original segment containing p, so there is no original boundary before p, so no. Good.\n\nThus evaluating each change O(1) get_end calls, no need max+2 theorem. Complexity O(n*26*get_end_cost). Great! This seems robust and simpler than uncertain DP.\n\nLet\'s test algorithm manually on examples.\n\nExample1 s="accca", k=2, n5.\nCompute original segments: start0, original_end? first a0,c1. 3rd? only a,c first positions c1,a0; (k+1=3)rd n => r5. So one segment L0,R5, count_from[0]=1.\np2, c=b(1). L=0,pre=0,start0.\nget_end(0,p2,c1):\nnext_pos[0]: a0,c1? s= a c c c a. first a0,b n,c1...\nold c. first old c=1 != p2, so removal no effect because c occurs before p. add b at2 if p< n. Distinct first positions: a0,c1,b2. k+1=3rd first[2]=2? Sorted [0,1,2] => return first[k=2]=2. So r=2. total +=1. r==p? p=2 yes, start=2.\nget_end(start2,p2,c1):\nnext_pos[2]: c2? original s[2]=c, a4. first old c=2==p => next_pos[p+1=3][c] =3? s[3]=c so 3. add b at2 -> first b=2. Other a4. First c after removal=3, a4, b2. allowed k=2 => (k+1=3rd) sorted b2,c3,a4 -> first[2]=4. r=4. total +=1 =>2. r>p (4>2), add count_from[4]. Original suffix from4 "a": count=1. total=3. pre0 =>3. output max3. Good.\n\nExample2 s="aabaab", k=3. n6. original_end k=3: only a,b distinct ≤3, r=6. count_from0=1.\nAny p,c. Suppose p2, c=\'c\' (new). get_end start0 p2 c:\nallowed3. Original first a0,b3,c n. old b at2? s[2]=\'b\'; first old b=2==p, remove to next b5. add c at2. First positions a0,c2,b5. k+1=4th = n (only3 distinct) => r=6. r>p => total=1+count_from6=1. no increase. If c creates total distinct a,b,c =3 ≤k. Good.\nIf k=2 (not example), could split.\n\nExample3 s="xxyz", k=1.\noriginal segments: L0? start0 original_end: first x0,y2,z3 => 2nd position y2 => r2. segment [0,2) "xx" index0,1. start2 segment [2,3) y, start3 z. partitions count_from0=3.\np1 change to a.\nL=0, pre0.\nget_end(0,p1,c a): old x. next_pos0 x0,y2... remove old: first old x=0 !=p (x at0 before p), so removal at1 doesn\'t change distinct x present. add a at1. firsts x0,a1,y2,z3. allowed1 => (2nd) first[1]=1? Sorted [0,1,2,3]. Return first[k=1]=1. r=1. total=1. r==p, start=1.\nget_end(1,p1,c a): next_pos1: x1,y2,z3. old x first=1==p => next x? none n. add a at1. firsts a1,y2,z3. allowed1 => 2nd first[1]=2. r=2. total=2. r>p (2>1), add count_from[2]. Original from2 "yz" k1: y,z =>2. total=4. Correct.\nFor p0 change? L0, p0. get_end(0,0,a) starts with modified a. first a0, y2,z3. allowed1 -> r=2? 2nd first pos y=2. segment [0,2) modified "axy?" Wait s mod p0 x->a: string "axyz". k=1. Greedy first partition should "a" (only a at0), next x at1? Actually if p0 changed to a, s="axyz": indices0 a,1 x,2 y,3 z. Longest prefix with at most1 distinct is "a" (r=1), not r=2. Our get_end returned? Let\'s recalc. s original "xxyz", p0 old x changed to a. Modified string: a x y z.\nFor start0, first_mod:\na at p0 ->0.\nold x: first original x=0==p, remove to next x at1. So first x=1.\ny first=2, z=3.\nAllowed k=1, need 2nd smallest first occurrence sorted [0(a),1(x),2(y),3(z)] => first[k=1]=1. r=1. I mistakenly said y2 but x next at1. Then loop r=1>p0, total=1+count_from[1] original from index1 "xyz" with k1: original from1? "x","y","z" =3. total4. Modified "a","x","y","z" =4. Correct. Output4.\n\nGreat.\n\nNow we need compute original_right for all starts. We can implement using `get_original_end(start)` with next_pos and sort. But get_end when p<start or no modification can use precomputed original_right. Need precompute before evaluating. Use function:\ndef original_end(start):\n    firsts = next_pos[start][:] maybe sorting. For n=1e4, okay.\n    firsts.sort()\n    return firsts[k] if k < 26 else n\nSince k<=25 after early k==26.\n\nCould compute original_right via sliding window more efficient but not necessary.\nNeed careful: `next_pos` list dimension n+1, each a list of 26 ints. Building with copies.\n\nImplementation plan:\nclass Solution:\n  def maxPartitionsAfterOperations(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n=len(s)\n    if k==26: return 1  # since all lowercase at most 26, entire string prefix all s distinct <=k, one partition regardless change? Changing still <=26, entire string always one partition. Yes.\n    arr = [ord(ch)-97 for ch in s]\n    INF=n\n    # next_pos length n+1\n    next_pos = [[n]*26 for _ in range(n+1)]\n    # Fill n row already n\n    for i in range(n-1,-1,-1):\n        row = next_pos[i+1][:]  # copy\n        row[arr[i]]=i\n        next_pos[i]=row\n    # original_right\n    original_right=[n]*(n+1)\n    for i in range(n-1,-1,-1)? original_end independent. Could compute any order.\n        firsts = next_pos[i][:] \n        firsts.sort()\n        original_right[i] = firsts[k]  # Because need (k+1)-th, index k. k<=25. If firsts[k]=n absent, r=n.\n    original_right[n]=n\n    count_from=[0]*(n+1)\n    for i in range(n-1,-1,-1):\n        r=original_right[i]\n        count_from[i]=1+count_from[r]\n    # segments info\n    seg_start=[0]*n\n    pre_part=[0]*n\n    cur=0; part=0\n    while cur<n:\n        r=original_right[cur]\n        # r > cur\n        for i in range(cur, r):\n            seg_start[i]=cur\n            pre_part[i]=part\n        part +=1\n        cur=r\n    original_total=count_from[0]\n    best=original_total\n    \n    # helper get_end, nested uses next_pos, arr, n, k\n    # For performance, avoid function calls? 260k* maybe okay. But nested helper with sorting.\n    def get_end(start, p, c):\n        if start >= n: return n\n        if p < start:\n            return original_right[start]\n        first = next_pos[start][:]  # list\n        old = arr[p]\n        if old != c:\n            if first[old] == p:\n                # next after p\n                first[old] = next_pos[p+1][old]  # p+1 <= n\n            if p < first[c]:\n                first[c] = p\n        # else no change\n        first.sort()\n        return first[k]  # allowed k distinct, (k+1)-th first at index k\n    # Evaluate\n    for p in range(n):\n        L=seg_start[p]\n        pre=pre_part[p]\n        # Could just evaluate all c 0..25. Include old/no change.\n        for c in range(26):\n            total=pre\n            start=L\n            # Since p in [L,R), p>=start. Loop max 2, but use while\n            while True:\n                r = get_end(start, p, c)\n                total += 1\n                if r > p:\n                    total += count_from[r] if r<n else 0  # count_from array has n index\n                    break\n                elif r == p:\n                    start = r\n                    # p unchanged <n. Continue. Need avoid if start>=n no.\n                    if start >= n:\n                        break\n                else: # r < p\n                    start = r\n                    if start >= n:\n                        break\n                    # continue\n            if total > best:\n                best=total\n    return best\n\nPotential issue: In simulation, after r==p and start=p, `get_end(start,p,c)` with start==p and p>=start (equal) works. It builds first from next_pos[p]; old removal if first[old]==p. If changed c not old, removes old at p, adds c at p. Good.\nIf r>p and r may be n, count_from[n]=0 already. `count_from[r]` valid since r<=n.\nIf r < p (shouldn\'t) and start becomes r, loop could eventually process p. get_end uses p>=start. Good.\nCould there be multiple r==p infinite? First time r=p, start=p; second call r cannot equal p because maximal prefix from p includes at least one char and for k>=1, (k+1)th first occurrence >p. If k=0 no. So fine.\n\nPerformance: For each p,c, get_end may sort list 26. 260k* maybe two =520k sorts of length26. Sorting small list in Python ~ maybe 0.5 micro? Actually 520k*26log ~ maybe 70M comparisons, okay. Building list copy from next_pos[start] also 520k*26. ~13.5M, fine.\nBut inside loop p*c 260k, simulation often one get_end then break if r>p. For changes causing r==p, second call. r==p occurs when prefix before p already has k distinct and c not in them. Could be many, but at most one per p,c. So max 520k calls.\n\nn=1e4; 260k can